Difference in Time Series

Differencing is a technique used to convert non-stationary data into stationary data.

Difference = Current Value − Previous Value (Lag 1)

Example

Original Data:

| Day | Sales |
| --- | ----: |
| 1   |   100 |
| 2   |   120 |
| 3   |   150 |
| 4   |   180 |
| 5   |   220 |

his data has an upward trend.

❌ Non-Stationary

=======
Apply Differencing

| Day | Sales | Difference |
| --- | ----: | ---------: |
| 1   |   100 |         NA |
| 2   |   120 |         20 |
| 3   |   150 |         30 |
| 4   |   180 |         30 |
| 5   |   220 |         40 |

Calculation:

Day 2 = 120 − 100 = 20
Day 3 = 150 − 120 = 30
Day 4 = 180 − 150 = 30
Day 5 = 220 − 180 = 40

Now we analyze the changes rather than the original values.

============

Python Code

-------------------
df['First_Difference'] = df['Sales'] - df['Sales'].shift(1)

# or

df['First_Difference'] = df['Sales'].diff()
--------------------

Why Use Differencing?

| Purpose                              | Benefit             |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------- |
| Remove Trend                         | Makes data stable   |
| Convert Non-Stationary to Stationary | Required for ARIMA  |
| Improve Forecasting                  | Better predictions  |
| Reduce Unit Root Effect              | Helps pass ADF test |


Differencing is a technique used in time series analysis to remove trend and make a non-stationary series stationary. 
It is calculated by subtracting the previous observation (lag 1) from the current observation

